# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values.
# .env.local is already gitignored — never commit real keys.
#
# Vars prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ are bundled into client-side JS and are
# visible to anyone who opens the site. Everything else here is
# server-only and must never be exposed to the browser.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — database (server-only secret + one public URL)
# Get both from: Supabase dashboard → your project → Settings → API
#
# Before running the app with Stage 4 code, also run
# supabase/migrations/0001_stage4_auth.sql against this project (SQL
# editor, or `supabase db push` if you use the CLI) — it adds the
# users.role / users.privy_user_id columns and the audit_log table
# everything below now depends on.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real RLS pilot (lib/supabaseUserClient.ts, migration
# 0017_orders_rls_pilot.sql) — only the GET routes under app/api/orders/**
# use these, everything else in the app keeps using
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above, unchanged. Both optional: unset, order
# reads fall back to the service-role client exactly like before this
# pilot existed (a console warning logs when that happens, see
# lib/supabaseUserClient.ts), so leaving these blank does not break
# anything, it just means the RLS second layer isn't active yet.
#
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET: dashboard → Settings → API → JWT Settings →
#   "Legacy JWT Secret" (a shared string you can reveal/copy). If your
#   project only shows "JWT Signing Keys" (asymmetric, no shared
#   secret), this specific approach doesn't apply, talk to whoever set
#   this pilot up before changing it.
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY: same Settings → API page, the anon/publishable key.
#   Safe-to-expose by Supabase's own design, but this app doesn't
#   expose it client-side, it's used server-only as the base client key
#   when constructing the per-request authenticated-role client.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Privy — wallet/email/Google login
# App ID: dashboard.privy.io → your app → Settings → Basics → App ID
# App Secret: same page, further down — server-only, never exposed to the
#   browser. Needed to verify access tokens server-side (lib/privyServer.ts).
# Verification Key (optional): same page → JWT verification key. Skips a
#   network round-trip to Privy on every request if set; the app works
#   fine without it, just slightly slower per auth check.
#
# Without a real NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID, the app shows a "Privy isn't
# configured yet" screen instead of the sign-in flow (it used to hard
# crash here — see components/Web3Providers.tsx).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_PRIVY_APP_ID=
PRIVY_APP_SECRET=
PRIVY_VERIFICATION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This app's own session — Stage 4. A random 32+ byte secret used to sign
# the httpOnly session cookie every protected API route checks (see
# lib/session.ts for why this app runs its own session instead of trusting
# Privy's client-side auth state directly). Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Rotating this invalidates every existing session — everyone has to log
# in again. Treat it like any other server secret: never commit it, never
# log it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reown (WalletConnect) — external wallet connection support
# Get from: cloud.reown.com (formerly cloud.walletconnect.com) → new project
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_REOWN_PROJECT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Circle — developer-controlled wallets, used for escrow release
# Get from: console.circle.com → Developer Dashboard → API Keys
# CIRCLE_ENTITY_SECRET is generated once during Circle's entity setup flow,
# not a normal API key — see Circle's developer-controlled wallets docs.
# ESCROW_WALLET_ADDRESS is the same wallet's public address (safe to expose,
# hence NEXT_PUBLIC_); ESCROW_WALLET_ID is Circle's internal ID for it and
# stays server-only. Only ESCROW_WALLET_ID is actually read by the app
# (lib/paymentProvider.ts) — the address is a reference value for YOU,
# printed by scripts/create-escrow-wallet.mjs, for funding it via
# https://faucet.circle.com and looking it up on a block explorer. No
# code in this app reads it back, so it's fine to leave blank if you'd
# rather just keep the wallet ID.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CIRCLE_API_KEY=
CIRCLE_ENTITY_SECRET=
ESCROW_WALLET_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_ESCROW_WALLET_ADDRESS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Yellow Card — NGN funding/refund legs (lib/yellowCardProvider.ts).
# REAL as of all three of these being set (bank-transfer funding only,
# see docs/payment-integration.md for why refund-support is restricted
# to that channel). Get API_KEY/SECRET_KEY from: Treasury Portal
# (portal.yellowcard.io) → API Keys → Create New, grant both "API read"
# and "API write" (collection request, payment request, settlement).
# The secret is shown once, save it immediately.
#
# ENVIRONMENT defaults to sandbox if unset — deliberately never defaults
# to production, set it explicitly only once you're ready to move real
# money. Production also requires sharing your server's static outbound
# IP with Yellow Card for whitelisting first (Vercel has none by
# default, needs a NAT/static-IP add-on) — a deploy-time step, not
# something this env var alone solves.
#
# Buyer funding also requires a buyer_kyc_profiles row on file
# (migration 0018_buyer_kyc.sql, POST /api/buyer-kyc) — these three env
# vars alone are not enough for a real funding call to succeed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
YELLOW_CARD_API_KEY=
YELLOW_CARD_SECRET_KEY=
YELLOW_CARD_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox

# Real escrow release (Accept delivery) now ALSO requires this, on top
# of CIRCLE_API_KEY/CIRCLE_ENTITY_SECRET/ESCROW_WALLET_ID below: the
# supplier's payout no longer goes to their own crypto wallet, it goes
# through Yellow Card's real Send API, which converts and pays their
# bank account (lib/yellowCardProvider.ts's createSettlementSend). This
# value MUST match the exact blockchain your ESCROW_WALLET_ID actually
# holds USDC on in Circle's own console — there is no way to verify this
# from code, get it wrong and real USDC gets sent to a network Yellow
# Card isn't watching for, which is unrecoverable. No default; the app
# refuses to attempt a real release without it once Yellow Card is
# otherwise configured.
YELLOW_CARD_ESCROW_CRYPTO_NETWORK=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8x8 JaaS (Jitsi-as-a-Service), optional, upgrades the live verification
# call off meet.jit.si's free public server. Without these three set, the
# call still works exactly as before, meet.jit.si's shared server just
# occasionally shows a "log in to become a moderator, otherwise wait"
# screen to whichever party joins an empty room first (see
# lib/jaasAuth.ts). All three set at once removes that entirely.
#
# 1. Sign up at jaas.8x8.vc, create an app. JAAS_APP_ID is shown on the
#    app's overview page.
# 2. On the same page, generate an API key. JAAS_API_KEY_ID is the key's
#    ID (the JWT "kid"), and download its private key.
# 3. JAAS_PRIVATE_KEY is that downloaded key's PEM content, with real
#    newlines replaced by the literal two characters \n so it survives
#    being a single-line env var (this app un-escapes it at runtime, see
#    lib/jaasAuth.ts). Example: "-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\nMIIev...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n"
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JAAS_APP_ID=
JAAS_API_KEY_ID=
JAAS_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JAAS_WEBHOOK_SECRET, optional, but the ONLY thing standing between the
# verification call's duration gate and two colluding accounts (or one
# attacker controlling both a buyer and a supplier login) faking a whole
# call with no real one ever happening — see lib/orderService.ts's
# applyJaasWebhookPresence and app/api/webhooks/jaas/route.ts. Without
# it, verification_call_seconds still requires BOTH parties to
# independently report overlapping time (lib/callVerification.ts), which
# defeats a single dishonest party but not two colluding ones.
#
# Registration is manual, not an API call: once JAAS_APP_ID/
# JAAS_API_KEY_ID/JAAS_PRIVATE_KEY above are set, go to the JaaS
# Console's Webhooks section, add an endpoint at
# https://<your-domain>/api/webhooks/jaas, select the PARTICIPANT_JOINED
# and PARTICIPANT_LEFT events, then click "Reveal secret" on that
# endpoint and paste it here (it looks like whsec_...).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
JAAS_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no admin env var anymore, on purpose.
#
# Stage 4 removed the old shared ADMIN_ID/ADMIN_PASSWORD gate entirely —
# it was a single hardcoded password (with an insecure built-in fallback)
# that anyone could also bypass client-side via devtools. The sourcer/admin
# role now lives in the database and can only be granted by an existing
# admin through PATCH /api/admin/users/[id]/role (audit-logged). The very
# first admin has to be set with a direct SQL statement — see the bottom of
# supabase/migrations/0001_stage4_auth.sql.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web Push — VAPID keys (Prompt 2 of the feedback/notifications pack)
# Generate a pair with: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY is server-only — never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never sent to the
# client. VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY is not secret (it identifies this app server to
# the push service, not a credential) but is still served at runtime via
# GET /api/push/vapid-public-key rather than baked into the client bundle
# as a NEXT_PUBLIC_ var — see lib/notifications/webPush.ts.
# VAPID_SUBJECT is a contact URI push services can use to reach you about
# this key pair (a mailto: link, or your site's https:// URL).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:support@sourcefi.app

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email fallback for critical financial notifications (Prompt 2) — resolves
# to a console.log stub (see lib/notifications/emailProvider.ts) until
# RESEND_API_KEY is set, same "real the moment credentials exist, stub
# otherwise" posture as CIRCLE_API_KEY above. Get a key from resend.com.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
RESEND_API_KEY=
NOTIFICATIONS_FROM_EMAIL=SourceFi <notifications@sourcefi.app>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cron secret (Prompt 3) — protects app/api/cron/order-timeouts. Vercel
# signs its own cron requests with this as a Bearer token automatically
# once both this env var AND vercel.json's crons entry are in place. The
# route refuses to run at all (503) if this is unset, rather than run an
# unauthenticated job that can open disputes and move money.
# Generate one with: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudinary — real image uploads (profile pictures, material listing
# photos, supplier verification documents). app/api/uploads/sign signs
# each upload with CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET server-side; the browser then
# uploads the file bytes straight to Cloudinary itself (this app's own
# server never touches multipart/file data at all, see
# lib/uploadClient.ts). All three are server-only, never NEXT_PUBLIC_ —
# CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET in particular must never reach the client.
# Get all three from: Cloudinary dashboard → Settings → API Keys.
# Until these are set, app/api/uploads/sign returns 503 rather than
# silently accepting an upload it can't actually sign.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLOUDINARY_CLOUD_NAME=
CLOUDINARY_API_KEY=
CLOUDINARY_API_SECRET=
